    {
      "id": "axes",
      "seconds": 14,
      "heading": "Axes",
      "narration": "Each axis has a number. In a matrix, axis one runs down the rows. Axis two runs along each row. Many operations work along an axis. In {X_eTaL|@xtl} a subscript after the name picks the axis.",
      "lines": [],
      "slide": {
        "type": "points",
        "steps": [
          0,
          0,
          1,
          2,
          2
        ],
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Axis 1",
            "sub": "down the rows"
          },
          {
            "text": "Axis 2",
            "sub": "along each row"
          },
          {
            "text": "A subscript picks the axis",
            "sub": "_1   _2   _3"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "reduce-axis",
      "seconds": 16,
      "heading": "Reduce along an axis",
      "narration": "Reduce works along axis one unless told otherwise. Writing the subscript one says so plainly. Both give five, seven, nine, the sums down the columns. With subscript two, it sums along each row instead. That gives six and fifteen.",
      "lines": [],
      "panel": {
        "type": "code",
        "lines": [
          {
            "xtl": "'+ r_/ M"
          },
          {
            "code": "5 7 9"
          },
          {
            "xtl": "'+ r_/_1 M"
          },
          {
            "code": "5 7 9"
          },
          {
            "xtl": "'+ r_/_2 M"
          },
          {
            "code": "6 15"
          }
        ],
        "focus": [
          [
            0,
            1
          ],
          [
            2,
            3
          ],
          [
            0,
            1,
            2,
            3
          ],
          [
            4,
            5
          ],
          [
            4,
            5
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rotate",
      "seconds": 16,
      "heading": "Rotate and reverse",
      "narration": "Rotate shifts items around. Along axis one, the rows trade places. Along axis two, each row shifts left by one. Reverse takes a subscript too. Along axis two, each row is reversed.",
      "lines": [],
      "panel": {
        "type": "code",
        "lines": [
          {
            "xtl": "1 o_-_1 M"
          },
          {
            "code": "4 5 6"
          },
          {
            "code": "1 2 3"
          },
          {
            "xtl": "1 o_-_2 M"
          },
          {
            "code": "2 3 1"
          },
          {
            "code": "5 6 4"
          },
          {
            "xtl": "r_ev_2 M"
          },
          {
            "code": "3 2 1"
          },
          {
            "code": "6 5 4"
          }
        ],
        "focus": [
          [
            0
          ],
          [
            0,
            1,
            2
          ],
          [
            3,
            4,
            5
          ],
          [
            6
          ],
          [
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "transpose",
      "seconds": 12,
      "heading": "Transpose",
      "narration": "Transpose reverses the order of the {axes|@axes}. Rows become columns. A two by three matrix becomes three by two.",
      "lines": [],
      "panel": {
        "type": "code",
        "lines": [
          {
            "xtl": "o_\\ M"
          },
          {
            "code": "1 4"
          },
          {
            "code": "2 5"
          },
          {
            "code": "3 6"
          },
          {
            "xtl": "s_hape o_\\ M"
          },
          {
            "code": "3 2"
          }
        ],
        "focus": [
          [
